{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"1420e137b2e053d690a5cb5884a550ebd7b5e002af96a7a21682333af13176cb","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_03bfd4ec8305b008cb21e33199ada68bf01599bde8e0cc5844b19c1b6b351502","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a technically sound and well-motivated investigation into the correspondence between human visual perception and the internal computations of deep neural networks. The methodological approach is rigorous, utilizing established psychophysical datasets and standard DNN architectures to test specific perceptual phenomena. The experimental design effectively controls for confounding variables, and the statistical reporting is transparent, providing a clear picture of the model's predictive capabilities relative to theoretical limits and prior baselines. While the work relies on existing datasets and standard implementations, the level of detail provided in the supplementary sections and the clarity of the metric definitions support reproducibility. The comparison to prior work is appropriate for the scope of the study, focusing on relevant perceptual models and DNN-based metrics.","paper_id":"BJbD_Pqlg","paper_identity_sha256":"4e36405c4c6fa67c1aca0019b3e4a5e6b27e82fa7b89ba918e1c64020a4f5b15","prompt_identity_sha256":"5f00d9fcc27fb4e5e8d8c586d69e85779021093a7c058c941b75f253eb17bdab","raw_response_sha256":"68da44a71d2d2fe6b13c33c44933f367ff4e467b63275375da80f97f568ebcee","request_identity_sha256":"4d5696d2a7ba7f993b72ec0cb9958bb8fdb0ec851cac7e5c26a5aab9043b657f","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"fb047d5c0786a9198df53d8fa251b7acc32c48d4f168f2a5c000c4bbf0941fb0","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-008","SRC-009","SRC-016","SRC-020"],"review_text":"The proposed methods are technically sound and mathematically consistent. The paper defines clear metrics for comparing DNN computations to perceptual data, specifically the L1 distance for image change sensitivity (Equation 2) and mutual information for context modulation (Equation 3). The use of mutual information to quantify target-discriminative information in isolated neurons is a standard and appropriate information-theoretic measure. The formulation of the perceptual threshold scale (Equation 1) aligns with standard psychophysical practices. The methodological choice to use reduced, non-natural stimuli is well-justified as a means to control for inherent task difficulty and prevent confounding with categorization consistency. No conceptual errors or mis-specified objectives are apparent in the core technical process.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-008","SRC-010","SRC-011","SRC-014","SRC-017"],"review_text":"The experimental design is adequate for the claims made. The study utilizes the Local Image Masking Database (Alam et al., 2014) for image change sensitivity, which provides a robust psychophysical ground truth. For context modulation, the paper reproduces stimuli from established psychophysical experiments on segmentation, crowding, and shape superiority, ensuring the validity of the perceptual comparisons. The evaluation metrics (R2, SROCC, RMSE) are appropriate for assessing predictive power. The paper includes ablation-like analyses, such as testing the robustness of the VGG-19 model to different metrics (L1 vs L2), noise levels, and image scales (Table 2, referenced in text). The comparison of DNN predictions against simple image statistics and a detailed perceptual model provides a strong baseline for evaluating the added value of the DNN computation.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-009","SRC-016","SRC-017"],"review_text":"The paper provides sufficient detail for reproducibility of the core analyses. It specifies the DNN architectures used (CaffeNet, GoogLeNet, VGG-19, ResNet-152) and the specific layers involved in the computation. The noise augmentation process is described, including the number of repetitions (10 or more) and the range of noise levels (-40 to 25 dB). The mutual information calculation details, such as the quantization of activations into eight equal-amount bins and the use of 500 samples, are clearly stated. While the paper does not explicitly state code availability in the main text, the detailed description of the metrics, data sources, and experimental parameters (referenced in Section 7.3 for configuration details) allows for a reasonable reconstruction of the experimental pipeline. The reliance on standard, publicly available DNN architectures and established datasets further supports reproducibility.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-005","SRC-006","SRC-010","SRC-029"],"review_text":"The paper positions its work effectively within the context of prior research. It acknowledges the convergence of DNN training regimes and the predictive power of DNNs for neural data (Cadieu et al., 2014; Yamins et al., 2014). The comparison to prior work is specific and relevant, contrasting the proposed L1 metric with simple image statistical properties (RMS contrast) and a detailed perceptual model (Alam et al., 2014). The paper also references related work on perceptual loss metrics (Gatys et al., 2015; Johnson et al., 2016) and situates its findings within the broader debate on feedforward versus recurrent processing. The literature review is comprehensive enough to support the claims of computational similarity without overreaching.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-010","SRC-013"],"review_text":"The statistical rigor is appropriate for the type of analysis presented. The paper reports the percent of linearly explained variability (R2), Spearman rank-order correlation coefficient (SROCC), and root mean squared error (RMSE) for the prediction models. These metrics provide a multi-faceted view of the model's performance, capturing both linear fit and rank-order consistency. The inclusion of the inter-person similarity as a theoretical maximum (84% R2) provides a meaningful upper bound for interpreting the DNN's predictive power (approx. 60% R2). The paper does not report confidence intervals or significance tests for the correlation coefficients, which is a minor limitation, but the large sample size of the psychophysical data (1080 images) and the consistency of results across multiple DNN architectures lend credibility to the findings. The statistical reporting is transparent and allows for a clear assessment of the model's effectiveness.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"245c867efc116f8ffd497f57ebe0614c8cd3678db275a6821f12b17b874a3418","paper_id":"BJbD_Pqlg","paper_ordinal":33,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"68da44a71d2d2fe6b13c33c44933f367ff4e467b63275375da80f97f568ebcee","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BJbD_Pqlg","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"aa157f80223ede65837ab9d8cae9d81a26b0adb64bf2938b6bfe76166cf8d278","request_identity_sha256":"4d5696d2a7ba7f993b72ec0cb9958bb8fdb0ec851cac7e5c26a5aab9043b657f","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":1404,"prompt_tokens":19127,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":20531},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"4d5696d2a7ba7f993b72ec0cb9958bb8fdb0ec851cac7e5c26a5aab9043b657f","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}